{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And five of you shall pursue, etc.</b> It is difficult: after the verse has said that the enemies will fall before us, etc., as a general principle — which implies even a myriad before two — if so, what room is there to say ״and five of you shall pursue a hundred״? This is nothing but a diminution from the first blessing." "However, the verses may be explained according to what they of blessed memory said in tractate Sotah, page 11, and this is their language: ״The measure of good is always greater than the measure of punishment.״ And the Tosafot raised a difficulty there at the words beginning ״always,״ and this is their language: ״And if you will say, behold we find a measure of punishment greater than the measure of good, for it is written (Deuteronomy 32:30) ״How should one pursue a thousand and two put ten thousand to flight״ regarding the measure of punishment, while regarding the measure of good it is written ״and five of you shall pursue a hundred״? And one may answer that regarding the measure of punishment it speaks of pursuit and not killing, whereas here it is written ״and five of you shall pursue a hundred״ and the end of the verse is ״and your enemies shall fall before you by the sword״״ — thus far their words. And it is difficult for me regarding their words of blessed memory from what they said in tractate Moed Katan, chapter אלו מגלחין (Moed Katan 16b), and this is their language: ״אֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹת הַגִּבּוֹרִים and so forth, עַל שְׁמוֹנֶה מֵאוֹת חָלָל בְּפַעַם אַחַת״ — that he would shoot an arrow and fell eight hundred casualties at once, and he would grieve over the two hundred, since it is written ״אֵיכָה יִרְדֹּף אֶחָד אֶלֶף,״ a bat kol went forth and said to him: ״Only regarding the matter of Uriah the Hittite״ — thus far. According to the words of the Tosafot, that what the verse said ״How should one pursue a thousand״ speaks of pursuit without killing, why would David grieve over not killing a thousand, since it is written ״How should one pursue a thousand״ — does not this verse speak of killing?" "<b>Therefore</b> it appears that both the verse ״and five of you shall pursue a hundred״ and the verse ״How should one pursue a thousand״ (Deuteronomy 32:30) speak of nothing other than killing. And what they of blessed memory raised as a difficulty, it appears may be resolved according to what they said in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״מִכֶּם — from the weakest among you, and not from the mighty among you,״ thus far. And there is a fine point to examine: why did he repeat by saying ״and not from the mighty among you״? Rather, the intention is that he comes to preclude, so as not to interpret his words as meaning even from the weakest — therefore he specified ״not from the mighty.״ And according to these words there is no difficulty, for in this verse He mentioned only the blessing of the weak among Israel, that five of them shall pursue a hundred, etc., and those who are weak like them among the nations of the world are not even arrayed for battle. And what the verse said, ״How should one pursue a thousand,״ speaks of the mighty, and his saying ״one״ intends to mean the unique one, which is to say the mighty man — and there was no need to state this degree regarding Israel, for it may be learned from the measure of punishment, since the measure of good is greater." "<b>And now,</b> two things did HaShem command for the good of His people Israel: the one, He set no limit upon them, and they are the mighty of Israel — and regarding them He speaks in a single verse: ״And you shall pursue your enemies and they shall fall, etc., by the sword.״ And if your soul is aggrieved to know a measure, go and learn from the measure of punishment, as it is said ״How should one pursue a thousand,״ and it is a great principle that the measure of good is greater than the measure of punishment, or at minimum equal, as the Tosafot (Sotah 11a) of blessed memory proved from that which they said: whoever engages in Torah for one day in the year, Scripture accounts it to him as though he engaged the entire year — and they proved from what is said in the portion of the Spies, etc. (Numbers 14:34) ״אֲשֶׁר תַּרְתֶּם אֶת הָאָרֶץ יוֹם לַשָּׁנָה״ and so forth — behold that there is no necessity for it to be greater, only that it not be found less than it in the matter; by necessity you must say that one of Israel kills a thousand at minimum. And for this reason David grieved over the completion of the thousand, so that the measure of punishment not be greater, for David was one of the unique ones." "<b>And you will find</b> in Divrei HaYamim 1, chapter 12, that the verse says: ״אֵלֶּה מִבְּנֵי גָד רָאשֵׁי הַצָּבָא, אֶחָד לְמֵאָה הַקָּטָן וְהַגָּדוֹל לְאָלֶף״. Behold, before you: that what the verse said — five hundred and a hundred to ten thousand — refers to the weak. And by this He revealed that what the verse said ״How should one pursue a thousand״ (Deuteronomy 32:30) intends to say the unique one, not those of lesser stature. And now we have found relief from the difficulty of the seeming superfluity of the first verse, which said ״And you shall pursue your enemies״ — which speaks of the mighty and stated no measure, for there is no measure for them: it is possible that one may pursue five hundred thousand, and at minimum one to a thousand as stated in the curse ״How should one pursue a thousand.״ And HaShem left the matter closed, for not all the mighty are equal, and not all the merits are equal, and not all the times are equal. It may be that one pursues a myriad, but at minimum one to a thousand." "<b>And your enemies shall fall, etc.</b> He doubled saying this thing twice. Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Torat Kohanim) that the intent is that each man shall fall by the sword of his fellow. And it appears further to say, since He made a distinction between pursuing five and pursuing a hundred, He added further to say that the same judgment applies if there be a thousand of Israel — the fall of their enemies will grow in proportion to the measure of the difference between five and a hundred, so too it will be between a hundred and a thousand. And this is His saying ״לֶחָרֶב״ (by the sword) — meaning, according to the measure of the count of an avenging sword."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {4 "The verse quoted from Divrei HaYamim corresponds to 1 Chronicles 12:14. The chapter reference סימן י״ב follows the traditional Jewish chapter division."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:10.633885906Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}